WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:05.167
(electronic music)

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.170
- So my name is Alex O'Keefe.

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:35.870
I am the 2018-19 Kress
Fellow in Art Librarianship.

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:37.860
So I work in the Haas Arts Library,

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.410
and I've been working on Ensemble@Yale

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:42.640
for my project as the Kress Fellow.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.340
So the project I'm presenting today

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:45.840
is Enesemble@Yale,

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.800
which is a longstanding
project before I got here,

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.690
a collaboration between the DHLab

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:52.790
and then the Haas Arts Library,

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:53.870
representative Lindsay King,

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.964
in order to preserve Yale's drama history.

00:00:56.964 --> 00:01:00.260
What we're doing is we
have digitized programs

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:03.040
from the 1920s through about 2016

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.010
that we are asking people to transcribe.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.660
So it's a crowdsource
transcription project,

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:08.493
which is really exciting,

00:01:08.493 --> 00:01:10.510
'cause it means we get to
work with people a lot.

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:14.910
And so we basically have
the actors and their roles,

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.260
and we're trying to get
people to transcribe the names

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.640
so that we have a data
set that we can later use

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.100
to maximize asking
questions about the Yale Rep

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.020
that we don't know right now.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.900
I think the most exciting
part of this project

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.510
has been working to
invigorate a volunteer base.

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:30.616
So when I first came on,

00:01:30.616 --> 00:01:32.580
the project was all set up and ready to go

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:33.650
thanks to the DHLab

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:36.170
and all of the efforts
that came before me,

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:39.170
but my big initiative has
been bringing in the people

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:40.650
and getting them excited
about our project.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:43.780
Since crowdsourcing requires
the people, (laughing)

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:44.680
a crowd, as we say,

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:45.970
the crowd in crowdsourcing,

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:48.560
we have been trying really
hard to create programming

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.540
and create opportunities
for people to come together

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:52.600
over these exciting materials

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.260
and learn more about the history with us

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:58.102
as we also produce the product
of data set for later use.

00:01:58.102 --> 00:02:00.880
So, once we have all of the
crowdsource data together,

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:02.780
we're working now on what
we want that to look like

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:05.730
for the population to
access it, essentially.

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:07.730
So we definitely are
working on adjusting it

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:11.170
into FindIt at Yale, which
is the Yale side of things,

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:12.003
but we also are hoping

00:02:12.003 --> 00:02:13.790
to do some more data
visualization options,

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:16.110
like using Gephi and
social networking mapping

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.700
in order to show more of the relationships

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.040
between these people and their lives,

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.343
again, from the 20s through about 2016.

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:24.240
So the conversations we're having today

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:25.920
have been really exciting to be a part of,

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.440
especially listening
to all of the speakers

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.340
before coming to the poster session.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.170
I'm hoping that I'll trade
off with my colleague soon

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:32.550
to walk around and talk to more people,

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.100
but it's just the energy in the room

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:37.180
as well as the diversity of
digital humanities projects

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:38.560
is just so exciting to see,

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.640
and to see how everyone is
maximizing their different areas

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:45.570
to create these really
interesting projects.

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:47.610
Collaborating with the
DHLab has been excellent.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.080
Everyone has been really helpful.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.560
We have lots of times we have
to troubleshoot together,

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.190
and everyone's very open and helpful

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.540
in every angle that
we've had and (inaudible).

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:56.460
So we've been very excited

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:58.473
to represent Beyond Boundaries today.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:02.660
- My name is Maya Levin.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:03.493
I'm a junior.

00:03:03.493 --> 00:03:04.940
I'm studying the History
of Science and Medicine

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:06.760
and Ecology and Evolutionary Biology,

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:08.300
but I'm here in my capacity

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.064
as a History of Science
and Medicine major,

00:03:10.064 --> 00:03:12.602
and I'm a third-year undergraduate.

00:03:12.602 --> 00:03:14.720
So I'm working with Dr. Carolyn Roberts

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:16.201
in the History of Science
and Medicine department

00:03:16.201 --> 00:03:19.270
to look at manifests
from slave trade ships

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:21.568
during the transatlantic slave trade.

00:03:21.568 --> 00:03:25.630
Looking at the Latin
pharmaceutical abbreviations

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:27.070
of medication names,

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:30.730
expanding them into usable Latin terms,

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:32.330
and then finding an
English vernacular name

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:33.650
for those medications,

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:35.230
and eventually turning
them into a database

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:36.280
that people can use to answer

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:38.150
historical questions across time

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:40.970
about medications both
explicitly in the slave trade

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:42.180
and more broadly.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.490
I'm also hoping to sort
of trace those medications

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.060
back to diseases that people
were expecting to encounter

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:48.200
during the slave trade,

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:49.740
as we can look at, sort of,

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:52.020
likelihood, costs, and
amount of medications

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.610
that people were bringing
on to slave trade ships.

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:55.820
Yeah, so lessons learned.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:57.660
I think that transcription
is something that is

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:58.950
really an important skill

00:03:58.950 --> 00:03:59.970
and that we shouldn't lose it

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:02.107
as digitization becomes
something really important.

00:04:02.107 --> 00:04:03.940
Because though it may seem

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.430
that this is sort of legible
and doable handwriting,

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:08.270
I'm not sure how much the
camera can pick it up,

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:10.810
it's actually not able to be read

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:12.860
by a digital tool at this point.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:14.740
So it still needs to be done by a human.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:15.850
And I think that's a skill

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:17.140
that is really important to keep up

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:18.260
as we think about the way

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.920
that digitization and
humanities will stay united.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:23.300
And then another lesson learned, really,

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:25.630
is that looking at historical medication,

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:29.030
it's important to sort of
take a lens of judgment away.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:29.870
I think it's very easy

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:31.470
to look at these medications and think,

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:33.270
obviously, they shouldn't
be giving anyone mercury.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:34.183
I don't know what you were doing.

00:04:34.183 --> 00:04:35.920
But actually, looking at the ways

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:37.880
that people were developing
medication over time

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:39.290
and thinking critically about that,

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:40.750
but also giving them a
little bit of benefit,

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:42.550
I think can give you insight

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:44.630
into people in this time period

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:46.100
and the way that they were
thinking about medicine.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:47.830
That's much more valuable.

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:50.150
So next step is really
expansion of the data set.

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:53.200
For now, we have about 75
entries in this format,

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.270
but we've only worked with two entries

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:57.800
from actual manifests.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:59.270
So expanding those,

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:01.180
hoping to find more
manifests in the future,

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:02.540
and then hoping to find other students

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:04.410
that can help expand this data set

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:06.363
so that it can be turned
into an interactive database

00:05:06.363 --> 00:05:07.847
that people can really use.

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:12.630
- So one thing, I think,

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:14.970
that appears clearly in
the Beyond Boundaries today

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.480
is that the field is, in general,

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:19.550
of digital humanities is evolving.

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:22.630
We are not presenting projects wholly.

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:24.040
We are discussing together

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:26.740
about what makes digital
humanities meaningful.

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:28.510
And that comes to a good time,

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:29.750
because I think the (inaudible)

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:32.620
needs to be reshaped and modified,

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:33.990
because a lot of things were

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:36.669
enough endless and cycling questions,

00:05:36.669 --> 00:05:40.048
that are now maybe a bit behind us,

00:05:40.048 --> 00:05:43.680
considering how inspiring
and how interesting

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:47.790
the question and the things
that were discussed today were.

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:49.990
So, that's a great, great...

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:53.140
I think there are great
hopes for this field.

00:05:54.167 --> 00:05:58.520
- The project was part
of a graduate seminar

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:01.797
focused on 17th-century
French literature (inaudible).

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:07.400
And the project was kind
of a three-week period

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.720
within that course in which we took

00:06:12.382 --> 00:06:15.413
basically a manuscript
of a 17th-century comedy

00:06:15.413 --> 00:06:19.080
and learned to text encode it

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:21.010
using (mumbling) standards

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:22.340
and then realized it

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:23.900
through a number of different interfaces.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:28.120
So each group decided to envision

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:31.293
the scene that they were
responsible for in a specific way.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:33.910
But we were also working with a team

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:36.337
at a university in Seattle.

00:06:36.337 --> 00:06:38.720
So we were Skyping with them throughout

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.173
and collaborating with
them on the project.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:46.100
- Translating the book
from the object books

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:48.600
to a digital version of everything

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:52.010
is also a way to reread the work,

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:54.230
the literary work, in a new way.

00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:57.800
It's not just putting the text online.

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:02.800
It's rethinking about our
relationship to a book,

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:06.166
our relationship to literary works,

00:07:06.166 --> 00:07:10.320
and it's a lot of new
questions and new topics

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.103
for us, our researcher, as researcher.

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:16.990
- So we were able to communicate directly

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:19.950
with our partners in Washington via Skype,

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:22.060
and so we met in the Beinecke Library

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:25.570
and went back and forth about
the work we did over email.

00:07:25.570 --> 00:07:28.740
So that kind of kept the workflow

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:30.963
pretty simple and functional.

00:07:32.249 --> 00:07:33.082
- [Interviewer] Would one of you like

00:07:33.082 --> 00:07:35.460
to walk us through the project?

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:38.843
- I can do it.

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:41.620
Okay, so, as Doyle said,

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:45.200
we all designed five different interfaces.

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:46.400
And as you can see,

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:47.920
they all look pretty different.

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:51.730
And I think that one thing that we learned

00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:53.720
is that your interface really changes

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:56.590
as a function of what you
want to portray in the text,

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:58.610
what's the most important.

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:00.682
So, for example, these top two,

00:08:00.682 --> 00:08:02.570
this one is done by myself,

00:08:02.570 --> 00:08:04.860
whereas Madison completed this one.

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:07.010
And these ones are, I would say,

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:10.470
much more about the
performativity of the text,

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:12.580
much less about maybe the text itself.

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:15.030
Whereas this one, which is Doyle's,

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:19.950
showcases variations across
different editions of the text,

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:22.119
so it's a much more,

00:08:22.119 --> 00:08:25.563
academic, we could say, edition.

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:31.840
Yeah, so we all chose different
scenes, and as Walid said,

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:33.460
we kind of had to read our scenes

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:36.330
to see what was the most important thing

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:41.330
content-wise or more structurally
to share about the work.

00:08:42.032 --> 00:08:44.920
- And you had to debate with your partners

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:47.168
at the University of Washington in Seattle

00:08:47.168 --> 00:08:49.652
to elaborate those interfaces.

00:08:49.652 --> 00:08:50.485
- Yeah.

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:53.080
So this one is animated.

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:55.350
It's designed to function
like a WhatsApp message,

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:58.990
so we made the choice to not
modernize the text at all,

00:08:58.990 --> 00:09:00.840
but rather, just to kind of show

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:05.840
how this kind of conversation
is still relevant today.

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:09.630
This specific text, or this scene,

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:12.520
is kind of about gender
roles in the 17th century

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:15.038
as they were conveyed in this play.

00:09:15.038 --> 00:09:18.740
So we have color coded the text messages

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:20.469
to show the different genders.

00:09:20.469 --> 00:09:22.970
And so we kind of envisioned this more

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:24.510
as like an animated interface

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:26.650
that could be used as a pedagogical tool

00:09:27.790 --> 00:09:31.750
to show how one can read
kind of at any level

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:35.620
of French or of expertise
of the 17th century,

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:37.370
how one could read this text today.

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:44.380
- So my scene deals
with a bourgeois woman.

00:09:45.580 --> 00:09:47.960
She's pretending to be from a higher class

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:49.473
than she actually is.

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:54.560
And she says all sorts of lies

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:57.870
about the way that she has a carriage,

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:02.493
and as the scene progresses, however,

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:08.480
the other characters see through her lies

00:10:08.593 --> 00:10:13.113
and the image that
she's trying to project.

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:17.760
And I was reading a lot about selfies

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:20.280
and, sort of, social
appearances on social media

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:22.240
and thinking through these questions

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:24.300
that were very present in the scene.

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:26.350
We think of them as contemporary.

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:28.010
So I wanted to show that.

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:32.330
It's sort of one of the
subtexts of the scene,

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:36.081
and I wanted to make that
apparent with a visual.

00:10:36.081 --> 00:10:38.724
So I chose the mirror,

00:10:38.724 --> 00:10:43.470
what would be like an iPhone today,

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:48.470
and showed her self image cracking

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:52.713
as all of her lies are
revealed to be lies,

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:56.730
and was really thinking about how

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:59.150
to make the subject matter more relatable

00:10:59.150 --> 00:11:00.773
to a contemporary reader.

00:11:05.290 --> 00:11:06.950
- My name is Azza El Siddique,

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:10.930
and I am a second-year MFA graduate

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:13.330
in the sculpture department.

00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:16.293
The project title is named Tirhaga,

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:22.410
and it is using neural style transfer

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:25.490
to generate images in the likeness

00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:29.133
of an artist that recently passed away.

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:31.269
I've learned a lot.

00:11:31.269 --> 00:11:32.590
(laughs)

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:34.760
I personally don't have a background

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:38.310
in coding or programming,

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:40.871
and so, I mean I've taken, like,

00:11:40.871 --> 00:11:44.727
online Codecademy classes (laughing).

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:50.840
But the DHLab has helped me so much

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:54.203
in order to kind of, like,

00:11:55.820 --> 00:11:59.290
set up the system and
teach me how to use it,

00:11:59.290 --> 00:12:00.850
and being able to use it

00:12:00.850 --> 00:12:04.280
and just constantly learning

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:08.003
while working with the programs.

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:11.742
Yeah, it's like, constantly new for me.

00:12:11.742 --> 00:12:12.930
(laughs)

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:13.763
Yeah.

00:12:13.763 --> 00:12:16.470
So, with like the neural style transfer,

00:12:16.470 --> 00:12:20.980
there's multiple ways that you can use it.

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:23.930
You can use, like, color scale

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:26.491
in order to shift colors around,

00:12:26.491 --> 00:12:30.540
as well as content and style weight.

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:34.530
So I found that to be really generative

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:39.217
in the ways that taking
certain content images

00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:45.900
and applying the style and
to play around with that,

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:49.660
to kind of start to understand
the program a bit more

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:52.173
and to understand what it's doing.

00:12:53.210 --> 00:12:55.750
Yeah, so, that was...

00:12:57.730 --> 00:12:59.680
Yeah, that was like ways

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:03.560
like learning through the program.

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:08.044
So this is a image, like,

00:13:08.044 --> 00:13:11.240
of the artist's painting.

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:14.350
So this is one of the paintings,

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:17.290
and this is another painting.

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:21.140
So what I had done is
use these two paintings

00:13:21.140 --> 00:13:23.552
as the style weights,

00:13:23.552 --> 00:13:28.552
and from there, I had taken
images from his iPhone

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:33.610
that are used as, like,
inspirational references,

00:13:33.610 --> 00:13:35.530
and Photoshopped them together

00:13:35.530 --> 00:13:39.430
in order to generate a new
image that would be like,

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:42.920
in the likeness of a potential painting

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:45.023
he could've created.

00:13:52.090 --> 00:13:54.410
So this is a painting of his,

00:13:54.410 --> 00:13:57.130
but also combining multiple paintings

00:13:57.130 --> 00:14:00.273
to kind of create different
colorways as well.

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:05.580
Just like, having access to the technology

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:07.120
and also its ability

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:11.750
to process the information a lot quicker.

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:16.750
So being able to have the images available

00:14:18.950 --> 00:14:20.673
in a shorter time.

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:25.193
Yeah, and just having the support as well.

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:27.290
Yeah, to like,

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:32.215
learn the program, and, yeah.

00:14:32.215 --> 00:14:36.186
It's been a really great experience, yeah.

00:14:36.186 --> 00:14:38.269
(laughs)

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:41.154
- My name is Adam Erickson.

00:14:41.154 --> 00:14:44.010
I'm an undergraduate student, senior.

00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:46.550
I'm majoring in Applied
Mathematics and Physics.

00:14:46.550 --> 00:14:49.292
I'm working with a professor
at the school management,

00:14:49.292 --> 00:14:52.800
and we are working on
building an interface

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:54.660
to view time series data.

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:56.800
And this time series happens to be

00:14:56.800 --> 00:14:59.350
topic modeling on the Wall Street Journal,

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:01.890
so its corpus of natural language.

00:15:01.890 --> 00:15:04.980
And it doesn't fit really well

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:06.920
as a visualization on paper,

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:10.111
so to really explore all the features,

00:15:10.111 --> 00:15:13.480
thought it'd be good to
build a web interface.

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:16.130
So we used React.js,

00:15:16.130 --> 00:15:17.595
which is a JavaScript library,

00:15:17.595 --> 00:15:19.320
(mumbling) Facebook open source project

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:21.003
for building user interfaces.

00:15:21.003 --> 00:15:23.500
We also used another open source library

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:26.120
from the Department of Energy
for doing time series data,

00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:28.443
which wraps React around D3.

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:31.840
Basically, because both React and D3

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:34.210
want to make updates to the DOM,

00:15:34.210 --> 00:15:36.380
Document Object Model, of HTML,

00:15:36.380 --> 00:15:39.493
and this package helps them
play nicely with each other.

00:15:39.493 --> 00:15:43.890
We do this so that then we
can load in the CSV files.

00:15:43.890 --> 00:15:46.300
These files are the time series data,

00:15:46.300 --> 00:15:48.370
so, you know,

00:15:48.370 --> 00:15:50.250
value at certain points in time,

00:15:50.250 --> 00:15:53.520
such that we could
someday feed in new data,

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:55.335
and we wouldn't have to
update any of the code.

00:15:55.335 --> 00:16:00.060
So we have a menu here
where you can choose

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:03.660
which time series you want
to appear on the plot,

00:16:03.660 --> 00:16:06.280
and this, we hope, could
be of arbitrary length

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:07.453
sometime in the future.

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:09.600
And you see, here,

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:13.510
I'll un-click them, some of them.

00:16:13.510 --> 00:16:17.630
But you see, it starts with
a totally blank canvas,

00:16:17.630 --> 00:16:21.650
which we'll get to in a second here.

00:16:21.650 --> 00:16:23.516
And then, as you start to select them,

00:16:23.516 --> 00:16:25.880
it begins to draw the plots.

00:16:25.880 --> 00:16:27.330
And as you mouse over,

00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:30.800
it tells you the value of the time series

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:32.060
at certain points in time.

00:16:32.060 --> 00:16:34.810
So because these are newspaper articles,

00:16:34.810 --> 00:16:39.200
each topic is a topic that
might appear in the newspaper,

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:41.750
and then the value for a certain month

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:44.875
is the article which most
represents that topic

00:16:44.875 --> 00:16:46.240
during that month.

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:48.265
So like the top headline, more or less.

00:16:48.265 --> 00:16:51.430
And you can add a bunch and overlay them,

00:16:51.430 --> 00:16:54.920
for example, here we have
the topic of police crime,

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:56.790
mobile devices, and internet.

00:16:56.790 --> 00:16:57.870
And you can mouse over them

00:16:57.870 --> 00:17:01.290
and see how they change
throughout time and compare them.

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:04.300
And then, if you select
them all on one axes,

00:17:04.300 --> 00:17:06.860
you can get some more absolute comparisons

00:17:06.860 --> 00:17:08.550
of their relative proportions.

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:13.100
So you might see a spike
right here in internet,

00:17:13.100 --> 00:17:13.933
and then think, like,

00:17:13.933 --> 00:17:16.770
"Oh, what kind of was
happening around that time?"

00:17:16.770 --> 00:17:19.220
And because all the proportions

00:17:19.220 --> 00:17:20.800
over all the topics sum to one,

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:21.633
you might see, like,

00:17:21.633 --> 00:17:23.294
oh, when this one went up,

00:17:23.294 --> 00:17:26.294
interest in the other one went down.

00:17:26.294 --> 00:17:27.793
Things like that.

00:17:29.930 --> 00:17:30.763
Yep.

00:17:32.610 --> 00:17:34.260
Yeah, so this is still prototype,

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:37.680
but one quick thing you can
do to check out an example

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:38.930
might be, okay,

00:17:38.930 --> 00:17:41.290
I'll choose a topic of the internet.

00:17:41.290 --> 00:17:43.810
So then you can see the time series

00:17:43.810 --> 00:17:45.502
and see, like, oh, in 1985,

00:17:45.502 --> 00:17:48.900
there was a lot less
discussion of the internet

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:52.510
in the newspaper than there
was right here, around 2000,

00:17:52.510 --> 00:17:53.880
the dot-com bubble.

00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:56.940
You can see there's some
articles about DoubleClick.

00:17:56.940 --> 00:18:00.060
I was alive at that time,
but not super conscious,

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:04.010
so I don't remember this,
but I know around 2000s,

00:18:04.010 --> 00:18:05.850
a lot was happening with the internet.

00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:07.870
And the one which really has a huge spike

00:18:07.870 --> 00:18:10.184
is if you choose natural disasters,

00:18:10.184 --> 00:18:12.970
which you can see even dwarfs
the spike in the internet,

00:18:12.970 --> 00:18:16.350
and this is right after Hurricane Katrina

00:18:16.350 --> 00:18:20.850
is when the topic proportion
really spikes in the newspaper.

00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:23.890
So, yeah, those are the sorts of things,

00:18:23.890 --> 00:18:25.472
trends you might like to look for

00:18:25.472 --> 00:18:28.060
and use this interface for.

00:18:28.060 --> 00:18:28.950
And really, what's nice,

00:18:28.950 --> 00:18:31.640
is you could choose an
arbitrary number of topics

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:35.693
and not have to worry about
how it updates the screen.

00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:40.322
Definitely, I learned a lot
about front-end development

00:18:40.322 --> 00:18:43.430
using JavaScript and React
and things like that.

00:18:43.430 --> 00:18:46.200
Asynchronous programming
especially is difficult,

00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:50.470
so if you're writing something
more low-level or back-end

00:18:50.470 --> 00:18:51.820
or like a project you might do

00:18:51.820 --> 00:18:53.640
in one of the CS classes here,

00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:57.960
you can kind of anticipate
how it's going to be used.

00:18:57.960 --> 00:18:59.490
But when you make a user interface,

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:02.212
you don't know in what order
the user might do things,

00:19:02.212 --> 00:19:05.352
so you have to build it such that

00:19:05.352 --> 00:19:09.108
clicks, drags, scrolls, whatever
could happen in any order.

00:19:09.108 --> 00:19:12.830
So you're prepared for anything

00:19:12.830 --> 00:19:15.310
the user might do to use it well,

00:19:15.310 --> 00:19:17.070
or even if they were trying to break it

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:18.442
in testing or something.

00:19:18.442 --> 00:19:20.430
So that was really difficult,

00:19:20.430 --> 00:19:23.560
and then that is what React helps do

00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:25.320
for building user interfaces.

00:19:25.320 --> 00:19:28.880
It really makes it a lot
easier to handle State

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:32.330
and to write the program, the interface,

00:19:32.330 --> 00:19:34.480
such that, oh, it doesn't matter

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:36.000
what order the user does things in,

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:38.390
because it's declarative.

00:19:38.390 --> 00:19:42.063
So I want to declare how
the front end will be used.

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:44.090
So yeah, a lot about that,

00:19:44.090 --> 00:19:45.635
and that was really interesting.

00:19:45.635 --> 00:19:48.552
(electronic music)

